  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a historical case study of mid-century US suburbanization, which may not directly reflect the dynamics of contemporary urban greening initiatives.",
      "what": "This historical case study analyzes how mid-century American redlining and racial exclusion of Black homebuyers were actively supported by a standardized domestic landscaping aesthetic. Promoted by banks, developers, and the federal government to stabilize property values, this green aesthetic functioned as a visual signal of racial exclusion and actively shaped a white suburban identity motivated to maintain segregation.",
      "why": "It challenges the prevailing assumption that urban greening is inherently positive and that environmental injustice is merely a problem of unequal spatial distribution, demonstrating instead how specific natural aesthetics can be engineered to enforce racial and economic segregation."
    }
